g/abs/2501.12512)\nciteturn3view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12512\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Mathematics > Geometric Topology\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 21 Jan 2025 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 29 Jun 2026 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:Aspherical 4-manifolds with elementary amenable fundamental group\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†James F. Davis , cite7†J. A. Hillman L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Aspherical 4-manifolds with elementary amenable fundamental group, by James F. Davis and J. A. Hillman\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF cite9†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:We classify the possible elementary amenable fundamental groups of compact aspherical 4-manifolds with boundary and conclude that they are either polycyclic or solvable Baumslag- Solitar. Since these groups are good and satisfy the Farrell-Jones Conjecture, one concludes that such manifolds satisfy topological rigidity: a homotopy equivalence which is a homeomorphism on the boundary is homotopic, relative to the boundary, to a homeomorphism.\nL17: We classify the closed 3-manifolds which arise as the boundary of an compact aspherical 4-manifold with elementary amenable fundamental group, generalizing results of Freedman and Quinn in the cases of trivial and infinite cyclic fundamental groups. Moreover, two such 4-manifolds are homeomorphic if and only if their \"enhanced\" peripheral group systems are equivalent, and each such manifold is the boundary connected sum of a compact aspherical 4-manifold with prime boundary and a contractible 4-manifold.\nL18: Comments:  | Minor rewrites and corrections; 30 pages\nL19: Subjects:  | Geometric Topology (math.GT); Algebraic Topology (math.AT)\nL20: MSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | 57M05, 57K41, 20J05, 57R67, 57K10\nL21: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:2501.12512 [math.GT]\nL22:    | (or cite11†arXiv:2501.12512v2 [math.GT] for this version)\nL23:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2501.12512†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: James F. Davis [cite13†view email ]\nL27: cite5†[v1] Tue, 21 Jan 2025 21:50:05 UTC (39 KB)\nL28: [v2] Mon, 29 Jun 2026 23:21:25 UTC (36 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Aspherical 4-manifolds with elementary amenable fundamental group, by James F. Davis and J. A. Hillman\nL35: \nL36:   * cite8†View PDF L37:   * cite9†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite14†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite15†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: math.GT\nL44: \nL45: cite16†< prev |   cite17†next > L46: \nL47: cite18†new | cite19†recent | cite20†2025-01 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite21†math L52: cite22†math.AT L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite23†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite24†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite25†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL61: \nL62: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL63: \nL64: [Button: ×]\nL65: \nL66: loading...\nL67: \nL68: Data provided by:\nL69: \nL70: ### Bookmark\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL73: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL74: \nL75: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL76: \nL77: Bibliographic Explorer (cite26†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL78: \nL79: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL80: \nL81: Connected Papers (cite27†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL82: \nL83: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL84: \nL