53: Both individuals and organizations that work with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nL154: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? cite41†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org .\nL155: \nL156: cite42†Which authors of this paper are endorsers? | Disable MathJax (cite43†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org )\nL157: \nL158: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite44†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL159: cite45†About†info.arxiv.org · cite46†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite47†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite48†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite49†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite50†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite51†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite52†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L160: \nL161: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://math.ucr.edu/~res/inprogress/mid0614.pdf)\nciteturn3view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 17; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://math.ucr.edu/~res/inprogress/mid0614.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 886\nL0@P0: Equivariant surgery, isovariant equivalences, and\nL1@P0: middle dimensional fixed point sets\nL2@P0: Karl Heinz Dovermann and Reinhard Schultz\nL3@P0: Department of Mathematics Department of Mathematics\nL4@P0: University of Hawai‘i University of California, Riverside\nL5@P0: Honolulu, Hawai‘i 96822 Riverside, California 92521\nL6@P0: ABSTRACT. Complete algebraic obstructions to equivariant surgery are de\u0002scribed in some cases satisfying a weakening of a standard dimensional inequality\nL7@P0: called the Gap Hypothesis. In such cases one has an extra obstruction beyond\nL8@P0: those which exist when the standard inequality holds. Equivariant homotopy\nL9@P0: equivalences can be deformed to satisfy a stronger condition called isovariance if\nL10@P0: the Gap Hypothesis holds, and similar results are obtained for examples satisfying\nL11@P0: the types of conditions assumed in this paper.\nL12@P0: During the nineteen seventies T. Petrie and others observed that many basic techniques\nL13@P0: of geometric topology – most notably those from the theory of surgery on manifolds – can\nL14@P0: be applied very effectively to manifolds with group actions if one assumes a condition called\nL15@P0: the Gap Hypothesis (cf. [DoS2, p. 20]). If the group G in question is cyclic of prime order,\nL16@P0: then the condition is dim X > 2 dim XG + 1, where as usual XG denotes the fixed point set.\nL17@P0: Adjacent cases satisfying the conditions dim X = 2 dim XG + ε (where ε = 0, 1) have been\nL18@P0: studied successfully in several contexts (cf. [Sc4, §II.4]). In particular if G is the cyclic group\nL19@P0: Z2 then results of the first author [D1] show that some results involving the Gap Hypothesis\nL20@P0: extend to cases where dim X = 2 dim XG with somewhat different proofs but other results\nL21@P0: do not extend at all. The purpose of this paper is to consider actions of G = Zp where p is\nL22@P0: an odd prime and dim X = 2 dim XG. In many respects the conclusions are similar to those\nL23@P0: in cases where dim X ≥ 2 dim XG + 2, but we shall also show that the self intersection of the\nL24@P0: fixed point set defines a new equivariant surgery invariant that has no counterpart in cases\nL25@P0: where the Gap Hypothesis holds.\nL26@P0: Summary of results\nL27@P0: Equivariant surgery proceeds by a sequence of steps, assuming that a map is an equivalence\nL28@P0: of the desired type over a distinguished subset and analyzing the possibility of converting the\nL29@P0: map to an equivalence of the desired type over a larger distinguished subset. In particular,\nL30@P0: for actions of Zp the first step is to convert the map into an equivalence over the fixed point\nL31@P0: set and the second step is to assume the map is already an equivalence there and measure the\nL32@P0: obstruction to completing the next step. From this viewpoint our main result can be stated\nL33@P0: as follows:\nL34@P0: Version #2.0.0, June 2006\nL35@P0-1: 12\nL36@P1: Theorem I. Let G = Zp where p is an odd prime, let f : M → X be a degree 1 equivariant\nL37@P1: normal map of smooth 1-connected G-manifolds of dimension 4n ≥ 8 such that MG and\nL38@P1: XG are closed 2n-manifolds, and assume that the associated map f\nL39@P1: G of fixed point sets is a\nL40@P1: homotopy equivalence. Then there is a well defined class σ(f) in the homotopy Wall group\nL41@P1: L\nL42@P1: h\nL43@P1: 4n\nL44@P1: (G) such that f is G-normally cobordant to a G-homotopy equivalence rel fixed point sets\nL45@P1: if and only if σ(f) = 0 and the self intersection numbers of MG and XG are equal.\nL46@P1: The statement of the theorem suggests that the vanishing of σ(f) does not imply that\nL47@P1: the self intersection numbers of XG and MG are equal; specific examples with σ(f) = 0\nL48@P1: but unequal self intersection numbers are constructed in Section 4. Theorem I has immedi\u0002ate applications to equivariant codimension one splitting theorems and isovariant homotopy\nL49@P1: equivalences that are parallel to results of [Sc3] in the case G = Z2. The full statements and\nL50@P1: proofs will be given in Section 2 (e.g., see Theorems II and 2.1–2.2).\nL51@P1: Since the main result deals with equivariant surgery under a condition that is a slight\nL52@P1: weakening of the Gap Hypothesis, the most direct approach to proving Theorem I is to begin\nL53@P1: with the ideas that succeed if the Gap Hypothesis holds and to modify the argument as\nL54@P1: necessary. The invariant σ(f) is the obstruction one would obtain if the Gap Hypothesis\nL55@P1: were true, and in our case it can be defined using results on periodicity in equivariant surgery\nL56@P1: theory from [DoS2, Ch. III]. On the other hand, the need to consider intersection (and self\n"
